// An `extern fn`'s body is C, so no gate inside it can speak; `@heap` is how its declaration says the C touches
// the heap (KR-47). The call is then a fact like any allocation the compiler writes, and a `@noheap` region
// that reaches it is refused through the chain. Before `@heap`, a C function that allocated was invisible to
// the proof, however directly it was called.
//
// `calloc` rather than `malloc`: the prelude's own `extern fn malloc` is `@heap` already, and this fixture is
// about the attribute on a declaration the author wrote.
extern "<stdlib.h>";
@heap extern fn UnsafePtr calloc(usize n, usize size);
unsafe fn int32 grab() { UnsafePtr p = calloc(n: 1, size: 4); return 0; }
@noheap fn int32 tick() { return grab(); }
fn int32 main() { return tick(); }
